<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oise (department)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oise is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Hauts-de-France region, created in 1790 and named after the river Oise. Its prefecture is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, and it includes Compiègne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> and Chantilly. In ancient times its lands were home to the Gallic Bellovaci and Silvanectes, and they later formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oise-department.jpg" alt="Oise (department)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chatsam · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prefecture_beauvais_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> lies in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with its southern border 35 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It is bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Somme, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>, Seine-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, Val-d'Oise, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a> and Seine-Maritime, and it touches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> to the south and Normandy to the west. It has belonged to the region of Hauts-de-France since 1 January 2016; until 31 December 2015 it was part of the former region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>. Its inhabitants are called Oisiens or Isariens, after Isara, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, on 4 March 1790, from parts of the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>. After the coalition victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>, British troops occupied the department between June 1815 and November 1818.</p><p>In the late 19th century two of its best-known monuments were rebuilt: the Château de Pierrefonds by Eugène Viollet-le-Duc at the behest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, and the Château de Chantilly by Honoré Daumet for Prince Henri, Duke of Aumale. When the Duke died in 1897 he bequeathed the collections of Chantilly to the Institut de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which opened the Musée Condé in the castle. Both the Armistice of 11 November 1918 and the Armistice of 22 June 1940 were signed in Oise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture and most populous commune is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, and the population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> was 829,899 in 2023. Its main attractions are the Parc Astérix theme park, opened in 1989, Beauvais Cathedral, the Château de Pierrefonds and the art collection of the Château de Chantilly, one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>Auvers-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a>, a village on the river, was visited by several impressionist artists, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a> spent his last 70 days there and is buried there beside his brother Theo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bellovaci lived in the modern <b>Oise</b> and Picardy around Beauvais.</blockquote><a href="/state/bellovaci">Bellovaci</a></li></ul></section>
